        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Metabolic Gas Analyzer (MGAS) is a pulmonary gas monitor that tracks breath-by-breath changes in the expired and inspired levels of oxygen and carbon dioxide during exercise.  
<P>
The MGAS base unit requires accessory hardware to perform measurement functions.  These accessories include the Gas Tank Module (GTM), a laptop computer, a 3-liter calibration syringe, mouthpiece headgear, MGAS accessory kits, Pall air filters, and rebreathe bags.  Depending on the type of test being performed, an exercise device (ergometer, rower, treadmill) can be attached.
<P>
The operation of the MGAS is based on the detection of oxygen by a rapid-responding zirconium oxygen analyzer and an infrared carbon dioxide sensor.  The oxygen sensor must be stabilized to a temperature of approximately 700 degrees C prior to running a rest.  The display screen on the external computer will indicate when the oxygen sensor has sufficiently warmed up.  The response time for gas concentration sensing is no greater than 130 milliseconds.  Volumetric flow is measured by using a disposable Pneumotach in conjunction with pressure sensors within the MGAS.  Flow can be accurately determined for a range of 10 to 200 liters per minute.
<P>
The MGAS is controlled by an external laptop computer (IBM, ThinkPad 750C).  The computer not only controls the MGAS operation, but it also performs calculations on the data, provides a user status and display interface, and stores data on the internal hard disk.  The software program provides a high degree of automated system operation wherein the crew performs minimal manipulations to control the MGAS.
<P>
The MGAS weighs approximately 9.7 kilograms.  Its dimensions are 49.0 (depth) X 25.0 (width) X 25.3 (height) centimeters.  The volume is 30,993 cubic centimeters.<END>
        </div>

